\h Yob
\toc1 Iyyov (Job)
\toc2 Iyyov
\toc3 Iyyov
\mt1 Iyyov
\mt2 (Job)
\is1 Introduction
\ip \bk The account of Iyyov\bk* (but commonly as known as ‘Job’ which is the English evolution of the Germanic adaptation of the Greek adaptation Ἰώβ (Iōb) of his actual Hebrew name ‘אִיּוֹב’) is an account about a godly man who was struck by a series of tragedies: all his children and their families were killed, all his extensive wealth was lost, and then he was struck with a terrible disease. By means of this seven-part account, the reader is informed about how Iyyov and his friends react concerning those tragedies. Then finally, God reveals himself to Iyyov and the others.
\ip Near the beginning of this document, God is told by Satan that it's not good governing of his people if he always rewards those who obey him. What if people only obey God because they're after the material blessings that would always follow. This would mean that their obedience no longer comes from their hearts. Yes, that's what Satan said about Iyyov, so God allowed Satan to test Iyyov's heart to see if he'd continue to trust God in times of suffering and misery.
\ip Iyyov's traditional friends assumed that he must be suffering because he sinned. Surely he wouldn't suffer all that if he'd been honest and righteous. None of them could understand why God might allow sickness or disease to strike someone if he was truly a righteous person, but this is too simplistic for Iyyov to accept. Therefore he challenges God to present his case. In all of that, Iyyov's faith in God isn't lost, but he desires to regain his honour as an honest and godly person.
\ip God doesn't answer Iyyov's questions but he responds by displaying his own power and wisdom and knowledge. So then Iyyov acknowledges God as the wise creator and requests forgiveness for his angry words.
\ip The final segment of this account explains how Iyyov was restored to health, peace, and prosperity (all the elements of ‘shalom’/‘שָׁלוֹם’), and how he actually prospered even more than before. It also shows how God was angry at Iyyov's friends, because they had so much to say when none of them actually knew why Iyyov had suffered—only Iyyov had really sensed that God and his eventual (but eternal) justice is greater than simplistic proverbs and trite beliefs.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 SECTION ONE: Introduction \ior 1:1–2:13\ior*
\io1 SECTION TWO: Dialogues between Iyyov and his friends \ior 3:1–27:23\ior*
\io2 a. Iyyov's complaint \ior 3:1-26\ior*
\io2 b. The first dialogue \ior 4:1–14:22\ior*
\io2 c. The second dialogue \ior 15:1–21:34\ior*
\io2 d. The third dialogue \ior 22:1–27:23\ior*
\io1 SECTION THREE: Praising wisdom \ior 28:1-28\ior*
\io1 SECTION FOUR: Iyyov's final statement \ior 29:1–31:40\ior*
\io1 SECTION FIVE: Elihu's speech \ior 32:1–37:24\ior*
\io1 SECTION SIX: Yahweh answers Iyyov \ior 38:1–42:6\ior*
\io1 SECTION SEVEN: The final restoration \ior 42:7-17\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\ms1 SECTION ONE: The introduction
\mr (1:1–2:13)
\s1 Iyyov's prosperous life
\rem /s1 Prologue; Job and His Family; Satan Tests Job
\p
\v 1 There was \add once\add* a man called Iyyov¦232252 \add (Job)\add* who lived in¦232249 the Uts¦232251 \add !(Uz¦232251)\add* region¦232249. \add He\add* was blameless¦232258 and righteous—both obeying God¦232261 and avoiding evil¦232263.
\v 2 He had seven¦232267 sons¦232268 and¦232269 three¦232269 daughters¦232270,
\v 3 and he owned 7,000 sheep¦232277, 3,000 camels¦232280, 1,000 cattle, and 500 donkeys¦232288, as well as many slaves. Iyyov was¦232292 \add richer\add* than \add anyone else\add* who lived in the area east¦232300 \add of the Jordan River\add*.
\v 4 \add Iyyov's\add* sons¦232303 \add regularly\add* held feasts¦232305 in their¦232312 homes, and whenever any of them held a feast¦232305, he would invite¦232310 all his¦232308 brothers and sisters¦232312 to¦232311 share the¦232311 meal with¦232316 him.
\v 5 \add Then once a feast was over\add*, Iyyov would send¦232323 \add for his children\add* and consecrate them—he got up early¦232326 in the morning¦232327 and offered¦232328 burnt¦232329 offerings¦232329 for each¦232331 of¦232330 them, because¦232319 Iyyov said¦232333, “Perhaps¦232335 my \add children\add* have sinned¦232336 and cursed\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Literally ‘blessed’ but it's possible that the original reading was ‘cursed’ and that scribes changed it to ‘blessed’ in order to avoid the uncomfortable concept of a person cursing God. Traditional manuscripts of the Hebrew Bible don't have a marginal notation about this (as they do in the case of 7:20), but LXX uses ‘ἐνενοήθησαν κακά’ which can be rendered as ‘have thought evil things’ and the Peshitta (Syriac) uses ‘reviled’. Many other translations also use ‘cursed’ since this is the kind of change that scribes are known to have made in similar cases.\f* God¦232339 in their¦232340 hearts¦232340.” \add ≈It was Iyyov's¦232324 regular habit to\add* \add intercede for his children\add*.
\s1 Satan's first attack on Iyyov
\rem /s1 Attack on Job's Character; Job's First Test
\p
\v 6 It¦232349 was the day¦232350 that God's sons¦232352\f + \fr 1:6 \ft It's not clear from the text who these are.\f* came¦232351 to present¦232354 themselves¦232354 to Yahweh¦232357, and¦232358 the adversary¦232361 (Satan) was also there among¦232362 them.\x + \xo 1:6: \xt Gen 6:2.\x*
\v 7 Yahweh¦232365 asked him, “Where have you¦232370 just come from?”
\p “I have come from the earth¦232378, where¦232369 I was travelling around¦232379 \add to see what was going on\add*.” Satan replied¦232364.
\p
\v 8 “Have¦232387 you¦232387 \add noticed\add* my¦232391 servant¦232391 Iyyov¦232392?” Yahweh¦232383 asked. “There's¦232394 no one else like¦232395 him¦232395 on¦232396 the¦232396 \add whole\add* earth¦232396—a blameless¦232398 and righteous man¦232397 who obeys me God¦232401 and avoids \add anything\add* evil¦232403.”
\p
\v 9 “Does Iyyov¦232413 \add get\add* nothing¦232411 \add back\add* for respecting God¦232414?” Satan answered¦232405 Yahweh¦232409.
\v 10 “Don't you \add constantly protect\add* him¦232421 and his household¦232424 and everything¦232431 that's¦232428 his? You've blessed¦232434 \add what he works on\add*, and his \add livestock have multiplied prolifically\add*.
\v 11 However, just stretch¦232440 out¦232440 your hand¦232443 and¦232439 \add ruin\add* everything he has, then¦232439 \add we'll see that\add* he'll¦232456 curse¦232456 you¦232456 to¦232449 your face¦232455.”
\p
\v 12 “Listen, \add I'll cede you control over\add* everything he¦232458 has,” Yahweh replied¦232458. “Only¦232470 \add just\add* don't¦232472 do¦232472 \add anything\add* against \add Iyyov himself\add*.” \add So\add* the¦232477 adversary¦232477 went¦232476 away from¦232478 Yahweh's¦232459 \add presence\add*.
