\p
\v 11 \add When\add* Iyyov's¦232858 three¦232855 friends¦232857 heard¦232854 \add about all the troubles that had happened to\add* him, they¦232866 each¦232867 came¦232866 from his¦232868 place¦232868: Elifaz¦232869 from Teman¦232870, Bildad¦232871 from Shuah, and Zofar¦232873 from Naamah¦232874. They¦232875 gathered together¦232876 to come¦232864 to console¦232878 and comfort¦232881 him.
\v 12 \add but when\add* they saw him from¦232887 a distance¦232887, they \add barely\add* recognised¦232889 him. They \add wailed loudly\add*, and they \add ripped their own clothes\add*, and they threw¦232896 dust¦232897 \add into the air so that it would fall\add* on their heads¦232900. \add (All three of those were traditional signs of mourning.)\add*
\v 13 Then¦232903 they sat with¦232904 him¦232904 \add there\add* on the ground¦232905 for seven days¦232907 and¦232903 seven nights¦232909 without saying a \add single\add* word¦232914 to¦232913 \add Iyyov\add* because¦232915 they had seen¦232916 that¦232917 \add his\add* suffering¦232920 was¦232919 extreme.
